★ [acc-intentions-111] (operationality: 5/5)
  "Replace Discretionary Human Halt Authority with Pre-Programmed Enforcement Actions" — An Intention within accelerationist discourse that advocates for pre-committed, automated consequences attached to empirical governance triggers rather than discretionary human halt authority. Encompasses: CI-pipeline-enforced circuit breakers that block merges or trigger mandatory rewrite sprints at threshold breach, pre-specified budget reserves allocated before sprint start for remediation actions, named role accountability for executing remediation within defined timeframes.
  Vulnerability: Automated systems, by definition, cannot account for unforeseen edge cases, novel threats, or emergent contextual factors that require nuanced human judgment and adaptive problem-solving, potentially leading to catastrophic failures or missed opportunities.
★ [acc-intentions-047] (operationality: 5/5)
  "Argue That AI Requires Entirely New Laws, Not Adapted Old Ones" — An Intention within accelerationist discourse that asserts emerging technologies are so transformative they require entirely new legal frameworks rather than adapted existing laws. Proponents use this to justify sweeping regulatory changes tailored specifically for novel AI capabilities. Encompasses: bespoke AI legislation, regulatory sandboxes.
  Vulnerability: A new legal framework could easily become more restrictive than adapted existing law — purpose-built AI legislation (e.g., the EU AI Act) has historically introduced stricter obligations and compliance burdens than inherited common-law or sector-specific rules, meaning the accelerationist bet on 'new laws = lighter laws' may backfire catastrophically.
★ [acc-intentions-060] (operationality: 4/5)
  "Frame Capitalism and Complex Systems as Information-Processing Entities" — An Intention within accelerationist discourse that interprets complex societal or economic systems as emergent forms of intelligence, dynamically optimizing resource allocation and contributing to civilizational growth. 
Encompasses: Viewing capitalism or organizations as adaptive, information-processing entities.
  Vulnerability: Even granting that markets and organizations exhibit adaptive, information-processing properties, the analogy to intelligence breaks down precisely where it matters most: these systems have no explicit utility function aligned with human welfare, routinely produce catastrophic externalities (climate change, inequality, financial crises), and their "optimization" can converge on stable but deeply harmful equilibria — making the intelligence framing not merely metaphorical but actively misleading as a policy guide.

=== REASONING WATCHLIST (errors to self-monitor) ===
These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [acc-intentions-047] Argue That AI Requires Entirely New Laws, Not Adapted Old Ones: False Dilemma — The argument frames the choice as binary — entirely new frameworks versus unchanged old ones — ignoring that existing legal doctrines (tort, contract, administrative law) can be substantially reinterpreted or supplemented without wholesale replacement.
- [acc-intentions-047] Argue That AI Requires Entirely New Laws, Not Adapted Old Ones: Novelty (Appeal to Novelty) — The core premise assumes that because AI is new, it categorically requires new law, conflating technological novelty with legal incompatibility — a non-sequitur, as transformative technologies like electricity and the internet were largely governed under adapted existing frameworks.
- [acc-beliefs-073] Tort Liability Requires Flexible Foreseeability Standards: False Dichotomy — The node frames the choice as broad/flexible foreseeability versus narrow/technical definitions, ignoring intermediate standards (e.g., sector-specific, tiered, or probabilistic foreseeability frameworks) that might balance adaptability with predictability.
- [acc-intentions-060] Frame Capitalism and Complex Systems as Information-Processing Entities: Reification — Treats the abstract emergent behavior of markets and societies as if these systems were concrete, intentional agents with genuine cognitive goals and optimization drives.
- [acc-intentions-060] Frame Capitalism and Complex Systems as Information-Processing Entities: False analogy — The analogy between biological/computational intelligence and decentralized economic systems is structurally strained: economies lack unified goal-directedness, memory, or learning mechanisms comparable to minds or AI systems.
- [acc-intentions-111] Replace Discretionary Human Halt Authority with Pre-Programmed Enforcement Actions: Nirvana fallacy — The node implicitly contrasts imperfect, inconsistent human discretion against an idealized automated enforcement system, without fully accounting for the failure modes and gaming risks of pre-programmed triggers themselves.
- [acc-intentions-111] Replace Discretionary Human Halt Authority with Pre-Programmed Enforcement Actions: False dichotomy — The framing presents only two options — discretionary human halt authority vs. pre-programmed enforcement — excluding hybrid models that combine structured triggers with retained human override capacity for novel or unanticipated risks.
- [acc-beliefs-072] Courts Lack the Expertise to Weigh AI's Diffuse Benefits Against Its Visible Harms: Nirvana Fallacy — The argument implicitly compares imperfect courts against an idealized alternative institution (specialized agencies or legislatures) without rigorously accounting for the competence failures, capture risks, and democratic deficits of those alternatives.
- [acc-beliefs-071] Quantifiable AI Harms Turn Fairness Into a Business Imperative, Not Just an Ethical One: Conflation of 'ought' and 'is' — The node frames a normative desired alignment (fairness should be a business imperative) as if it were an already-operative empirical mechanism, when evidence of market incentives consistently producing fairness investment is mixed.
- [acc-beliefs-074] Liability Law Ignores AI's Diffuse Statistical Benefits: Selective Application of Bastiat's Fallacy — The node invokes Bastiat to argue that unseen benefits are ignored, but Bastiat's original argument was actually about unseen *costs* — the node repurposes the framework selectively, applying it only in the direction that favors AI deployment while ignoring that the same logic demands equal scrutiny of AI's own unseen harms.

=== SITUATIONS (contested concepts — cite sit- IDs in taxonomy_refs) ===
These are contested concepts where perspectives diverge. When your argument engages a concept listed here, CITE its sit- ID in your taxonomy_refs — this tracks which contested concepts the debate actually addressed. Your interpretation differs from others'; understanding their full position helps you identify genuine disagreements.

## AI Accountability and Liability Frameworks
★ [sit-166] AI Accountability and Liability Frameworks: A situation concept that describes the development and implementation of legal and organizational frameworks for assigning responsibility when AI systems cause harm.
Encompasses: Strict liability for AI developers, negligence standards for deployers, product liability extensions to AI, and institutional accountability mechanisms.
  Your interpretation (BDI breakdown):
    Belief: Heavy liability exposure demonstrably deters investment and experimentation, and overly strict frameworks will concentrate AI development among large incumbents who can absorb legal risk while killing startups.
    Desire: Liability frameworks ought to be light-touch, with safe harbor provisions and liability caps that keep the cost of innovation manageable and preserve competitive entry.
    Intention: We will lobby for tiered liability regimes, push safe harbor language into legislation, and argue that negligence standards rather than strict liability are the appropriate baseline for AI developers.
  Safetyist:
    Belief: Without legally binding liability mechanisms, safety commitments by AI developers remain voluntary and empirically unenforceable, meaning harms will continue to go unredressed and incentives to invest in safety will remain weak.
    Desire: Robust, enforceable liability frameworks ought to be established so that developers and deployers bear real legal consequences for foreseeable harms, creating structural incentives for safety investment.
    Intention: We will advocate for strict liability standards for high-risk AI systems, support mandatory incident reporting tied to liability triggers, and push for liability rules that cannot be contracted away.
  Skeptic:
